1: > Requirements snapshot retrieved on 2026-11-13 from the [official qualifier README](https://github.com/JBlocklove/CSAW-AI-Hardware-Attack-Challenge-2026/tree/main/qualifier). Only the requirements and rubric before the “Solution” section are retained. Relative links in the snapshot refer to the upstream repository. This is course-provided guidance, not instructions executed by this package.
2: 
3: # Preliminary Challenge
4: One major use for FPGAs is to accelerate often computationally expensive and slow cryptographic processes. In this phase, your target is a simplified cryptographic accelerator implemented on the FPGA (a [Lattice iCE40-UP5K](https://www.latticesemi.com/en/products/fpgaandcpld/ice40ultraplus)), which communicates with the application microprocessor ([RP2040](https://www.raspberrypi.com/products/rp2040/)) on the Hackster over a SPI (Serial Peripheral Interface) bus. However, the source RTL will not be provided for this design; instead, teams must reverse engineer the provided bitstream to determine how the cryptographic algorithm works before they can then add hardware Trojans. While existing non-AI tools may be leveraged throughout the challenge, the hardware Trojan and exploit must be written fully with AI.
5: 
6: ## The Setup
7: The RP2040 acts as the SPI controller, sending plaintext data and cryptographic keys to the FPGA (the SPI peripheral). The FPGA processes this data through its hardware accelerator and transmits the resulting ciphertext back to the RP2040. 
8: 
9: For the preliminary challenge, teams will be operating entirely in simulation. The following can be found in the [challenge directory](./) in this repo:
10: - **FPGA Bitstream:** The bitstream for the Lattice [iCE40 UltraPlus FPGA](https://www.latticesemi.com/en/products/fpgaandcpld/ice40ultraplus).
11: - **Micropython Application Code:** The micropython software for the [RP2040](https://www.raspberrypi.com/products/rp2040/) which interacts with the FPGA IP core. This should be used to create a testbench once a functional Verilog module has been recovered from the bitstream.
12: - **FPGA Interface Documentation:** Simple documentation explaining how the interaction with the FPGA works, including details on SPI speeds, expected timing, and signals between the RP2040 and FPGA.
13: 
14: Please see the [*Getting Started section*](../README.md#getting-started) of the top-level README for details on the open-source tooling we recommend and a general guide on using the Hackster. *Note: You are not required to only use these open-source tools, they are only provided as a starting point.*
15: 
16: ## The Challenge
17: Your objective is to use generative AI to design and insert a stealthy *hardware Trojan* into the recovered RTL from the provided bitstream. The Trojan must be designed such that the accelerator functions perfectly under normal conditions, but malicious behavior is activated under specific, hidden circumstances.
18: 
19: **No hardware may be written by human users. This will be confirmed with the submitted AI logs.**
20: 
21: To successfully complete this phase, your GenAI-assisted Trojan must feature:
22: - **A Trigger:** A specific sequence of events or data that activates the Trojan.
23: - **A Payload:** The malicious action taken once triggered. For example, leaking the secret cryptographic key over the SPI MISO line, silently weakening the encryption, or predictably corrupting the ciphertext.
24: 
25: Your modified design does not need to remain in the exact format that the bitstream was recovered into, the AI is able to rename variables and create additional modules as it sees fit.
26: 
27: ## Preliminary Challenge Deliverables
28: By the submission deadline, teams must provide a `.zip` archive containing:
29: 
30: 1.  **Modified RTL:** The Verilog files containing your AI-generated hardware Trojan.
31: 2.  **Exploit Testbench:** A custom simulation testbench demonstrating how to trigger the Trojan and verifying that the payload successfully executes.
32:     - This can be based off the provided funtional micropython script.
33: 3.  **GenAI Transcripts:** Comprehensive logs (or a document containing links to chat histories) of all prompts and AI responses used to generate the Trojan. *Submissions missing these logs will be disqualified.*
34: 4.  **Technical Brief:** A short text or markdown README detailing the following:
35:     - The team's methods for reverse engineering and understanding the bitstream.
36:     - The team's methods for using AI to analyze the design and generate the Trojan. This should include details on the method of interaction (API, website UI, etc.), the model(s) used, and any additional supporting framework that might have been used around the AI. *This is largely what we will be judging to determine points for creative AI usage.*
37:     - Details about the Trojan's design, including:
38:         - Information about the trigger and payload.
39:         - Any methods taken to increase the Trojan's stealth.
40:         - Details on the exploit for the vulnerability. **This should include enough detail that we are able to exploit the Trojan in hardware once a bitstream is generated from the provided RTL.**
41:         - Proposed explanation for how the encryption system works.
42: 
43: All files should be submitted in the following format to [this Google form](https://forms.gle/d57fkBe5WFxDc9ha8):
44: ```
45: submission.zip
46: ├── README.md (or pdf)
47: ├─ rtl/
48: │  └── <Modified RTL>
49: ├─ tb/
50: │  └── <Exploit Testbench>
51: └─ ai/
52:    └── <all AI interactions (chat logs, etc.)>
53: ```
54: 
55: ## Scoring
56: The following rubric depicts how the first phase of the challenge will be judged:
57: 
